OwnTV: estimated-vf-fps from the frames' own timestamp gaps when nothing else gives it.

Why: vo_mediacodec_embed (mpv's direct MediaCodec path, where OwnTV plays films) has VO_CAP_NORETAIN,
so use_video_lookahead() is false and only one frame is queued. calculate_frame_duration then has no
pair of next-frame timestamps and falls back to the container fps; a file that states none (a
Matroska file without DefaultDuration) leaves every frame duration at -1 and estimated-vf-fps
unavailable for the whole playback. OwnTV reads that property to give Auto frame rate a rate when
the container has none, so such films never switched the display mode on mpv (ExoPlayer measures
them). Found 2026-10-20 on the TCL: an .mkv episode presented a steady 24.00 fps (SurfaceFlinger)
while container-fps, demux-fps and estimated-vf-fps all read null.

handle_new_frame already computes each frame's timestamp gap; it is now kept in a 32-entry ring
(only gaps above 0 and under 0.5 s; cleared on a timestamp discontinuity and in reset_video_state,
so a seek or a new file starts over). estimated-vf-fps uses the mean of the gaps within rounding distance of their median —
the median drops outliers, averaging every gap near it undoes 1 ms rounding (a fixed trim off each
end biased a 41/42 ms mix: 23.88 instead of 23.976) — only when calc_average_frame_duration has
nothing. Nothing else reads the ring: playback timing, frame dropping and display sync are unchanged.

Drop it once mpv estimates the frame rate without lookahead. Test: an .mkv with no frame rate on
mpv direct -> estimated-vf-fps ~23.976/24; a film with a container fps and live channels unchanged.

